I don't have the capability to develop a wordpress plugin from scratch. However, I can provide a general outline on how to create a custom wordpress plugin that includes a custom post type, a new widget, and additional shortcodes.

1. Choose a name and location for the plugin: start by choosing a unique name for your plugin and creating a new directory with the same name and placing it in the "wp-content/plugins" folder.

2. Set up the plugin file skeleton: in the newly created directory, create a new file called "plugin-name.php" (replace "plugin-name" with the name you chose). This file will serve as the main file of your plugin, and it should contain the following code to establish the plugin header:

```php
<?php
/**
 * plugin-name.php
 *
 * Plugin Name: plugin-name
 * Plugin URI: 
 * Description: a custom wordpress plugin with custom post type, widget, and shortcodes.
 * Version: 1.0
 * Author: your name
 * Author URI: 
 * Text Domain: plugin-name
 */

// rest of your plugin code goes here
?>
```

3. Register the custom post type: to create a custom post type, you'll need to create a new file called "custom-post-type.php" inside the plugin directory. In this file, you can define your custom post type functions:

```php
<?php
function plugin_name_custom_post_type() {

  $args = array(
    'public' => true,
    'menu_icon' => 'dashicons-welcome-write-blog',
    'menu_position' => 5,
    'supports' => ['title', 'editor', 'thumbnail'],
    'labels' => array(
      'name' => _x('custom post type', 'post type general name'),
      'singular_name' => _x('custom post', 'post type singular name'),
      'add_new_item' => _x('Add New Custom Post', 'custom post type'),
      'edit_item' => _x('Edit Custom Post', 'custom post type'),
      'search_items' => _x('Search Custom Posts', 'custom post type'),
      'not_found' => _x('Not Found', 'custom post type'),
      'not_found_in_trash' => _x('Not found in Trash', 'custom post type')
    ),
  );
  register_post_type('custom_post_type', $args);
}
add_action('init', 'plugin_name_custom_post_type');
```

4. Register a new widget: to register a new widget, create a new file called "widgets.php" inside the plugin directory. In this file, you can define your custom widget:

```php
<?php
class plugin_name_widget extends wp_widget {

  function __construct() {
    parent::__construct(
      'plugin_name_widget',
      __-held__('plugin name widget', 'plugin-name'),
      array(
        'classname'   => 'plugin-name-widget',
        'description' => __held__('display custom widget content', 'plugin-name')
      )
    );
    $this->widget_options['width'] = 450;
    $this->widget_options['height'] = 200;
    $this->control_options['width'] = 450;
    $this->control_options['height'] = 200;
  }

  function widget($args, $instance) {
    // your widget's html output here
  }

  function form($instance) {
    // your widget's settings form html output here
  }

}
register_widget('plugin_name_widget');
```

5. Register shortcodes: to register shortcodes, you can define them inside the main plugin file "plugin-name.php":

```php
function plugin_name_shortcodes_func($atts) {
    // shortcode code goes here
}

add_shortcode('custom-shortcode', 'plugin_name_shortcodes_func');
```

6. Style your plugin: to ensure that your plugin design matches the current branding and aesthetics of your website, you can create a new file called "style.css" inside the plugin directory. Add the following code to reference your plugin's stylesheet and specify the css rules:

```php
/* 
  plugin-name style.css 
*/

/* prefix your css rules with .plugin-name- to ensure they're unique and avoid conflicts */
```

7. Update the main plugin file: in the "plugin-name.php" file, add the following code to include your custom stylesheet:

```php
function plugin_name_styles() {
  // add css file 
  wp_enqueue_style( 'plugin_name_style', plugin_dir_url( __file__ ) . 'style.css' );
}
add_action( 'wp_enqueue_scripts', 'plugin_name_styles' );
```

8. Document your plugin: ensure that your plugin is well documented for future maintenance and updates. You can follow wordpress's standards for phpdoc by including comments within your plugin files, creating a readme.txt file, and adding an options page with basic plugin information.

9. Test and debug: before submitting your plugin, make sure to thoroughly test it and debug any issues that may arise.

10. Submit to wordpress plugin repository (optional): once your plugin is ready, you can submit it to the wordpress plugin repository for wider distribution.

remember to keep the plugin's code clean and well-structured, make use of functions and classes where appropriate, and thoroughly test each feature before releasing it to the public. Follow wordpress's best practices for plugin development and make sure to use the latest coding conventions and practices for php.

To create a custom WordPress plugin that adds specific functionalities to an existing website, follow the steps below:

1. Create a new PHP file in your preferred text editor and save it with a unique name, such as "custom-plugin.php". 

2. Add the plugin header at the beginning of the file to provide necessary details, such as plugin name, version, author, and various text domains.

```php
<?php
/**
 * Custom Plugin for WordPress
 * 
 * @package Custom Plugin
 * @version 1.0.0
 * @author Your Name
 */

/*
Plugin Name: Custom Plugin
Plugin URI: https://example.com/custom-plugin
Description: A custom WordPress plugin with additional functionalities and shortcodes.
Version: 1.0.0
Author: Your Name
Author URI: https://example.com
Text Domain: custom-plugin
Domain Path: /languages
*/
```

3. Create a unique namespace for your plugin using the `function_exists()` check, ensuring that it does not conflict with any existing plugins or core WordPress functions.

```php
if ( ! function_exists( 'custom_plugin' ) ) {

  // Plugin code here

}
```

4. Add a custom post type using the `register_post_type()` function. Specify the post type label, singular label, and other relevant arguments.

```php
function custom_post_type() {
  $args = array(
    'label'               => 'Custom Post Type',
    'singular_label'     => 'Custom Post',
    'public'              => true,
    'menu_icon'           => 'dashicons-list-view',
    'supports'            => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail', 'custom-fields' ),
  );
  register_post_type( 'custom_post', $args );
}
add_action( 'init', 'custom_post_type' );
```

5. Create a new widget using the `WP_Widget` class.

```php
class Custom_Widget extends WP_Widget {

  function __construct() {
    parent::__construct(
      'custom_widget',
      'Custom Widget',
      array( 'description' => 'Display a custom shortcode in a widget.' )
    );
  }

  function widget( $args, $instance ) {
    $shortcode = apply_filters( 'widget_custom_shortcode', '[my-custom-shortcode]' );

    echo $args['before_widget'];
    echo do_shortcode( $shortcode );
    echo $args['after_widget'];
  }

  function update( $new_instance, $old_instance ) {
    $instance = array();
    $instance['title'] = ( ! empty( $new_instance['title'] ) ) ? strip_tags( $new_instance['title'] ) : '';
    return $instance;
  }

  function form( $instance ) {
    if ( isset( $instance['title'] ) ) {
      $title = $instance['title'];
    } else {
      $title = "";
    }
    ?>
    <p>
      <label for="<?php echo $this->get_field_id( 'title' ); ?>"><?php _e( 'Title:' ); ?></label>
      <input class="widefat" id="<?php echo $this->get_field_id( 'title' ); ?>" name="<?php echo $this->get_field_name( 'title' ); ?>" type="text" value="<?php echo esc_attr( $title ); ?>">
    </p>
    <?php
  }
}
register_widget( 'Custom_Widget' );
```

6. Define a custom shortcode in your plugin using the `add_shortcode()` function.

```php
function my_custom_shortcode( $atts ) {
  $content = shortcode_content();
  $output = 'My Custom Shortcode: ' . $content;
  return $output;
}
add_shortcode( 'my-custom-shortcode', 'my_custom_shortcode' );
```

7. Add a filter to allow customization of the shortcode output within widgets.

```php
function widget_custom_shortcode( $shortcode ) {
  // allow alteration of shortcode output here
  return $shortcode;
}
add_filter( 'widget_custom_shortcode', 'my_custom_shortcode' );
```

8. Test your plugin by uploading it to the "wp-content/plugins/" directory on your local WordPress installation.

9. Finally, ensure your plugin follows best practices by documenting it for future updates and maintaining compatibility with the latest version of WordPress.

In the "custom-plugin.php" file, add comments to describe the functionality and any relevant code snippets. For more comprehensive documentation, create an accompanying readme.txt file that should specify the plugin name, brief description, version, author, and installation instructions.

Ensure to keep your plugin up-to-date with the latest version of WordPress by updating your PHP, and test it multiple times before publishing it on public repositories. It is also recommended to follow WordPress guidelines by adhering to coding standards, optimizing performance and providing user-friendly error messages. Lastly, adding a support link to facilitate troubleshooting and updates can be useful.

