        categories.put("Marigold", new ArrayList<>());
        categories.put("Chrysanthemum", new ArrayList<>());

        // Categorize each flower and add to corresponding list
        for (String flower : flowers) {
            String category = getCategory(flower);
            categories.get(category).add(flower);
        }

        // Print the categories
        System.out.println(categories);
    }

    // Method to categorize flower based on its name
    public static String getCategory(String flowerName) {
        if (flowerName.startsWith("Li")) {
            return "Lilac";
        } else if (flowerName.startsWith("Da")) {
            return "Daisy";
        } else if (flowerName.startsWith("Ma")) {
            return "Marigold";
        } else if (flowerName.startsWith("Ch")) {
            return "Chrysanthemum";
        } else {
            return "Unknown"; // Add default category for unknown flowers
        }
    }
}
```

This code uses a `Map` (formerly known as a `HashMap`) to store the categories and their corresponding arrays of flowers. We initialize the `Map` with the categories and empty arrays upfront, enabling us to access the arrays within the `for` loop without using `containsKey` method every time.

For categorizing each flower, we've separated out the `getCategory` method, making the code more readable and easily maintainable. We've added a default category ("Unknown") for any flowers that don't match the existing categories.

